17.text:
Add the plugin to vite.config.ts and give it a site block.
Nicely enough, that’s the minimum effort you have to do, as everything else gets sensible defaults.
Please do fill out the rest though haha, it’s worth it.
import { defineConfig } from &quot;vite&quot;;
import { seo } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta&quot;;

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    seo({
      site: {
        url: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
        name: &quot;My Site&quot;,
        title: &quot;My Site&quot;,
        titleTemplate: &quot;%s | My Site&quot;,
        description: &quot;My awesome site&quot;,
      },
    }),
  ],
});

Per-page overrides
Put a seo.config.ts next to vite.config.ts and the plugin loads it automatically, no questions asked.
import { defineSeoConfig } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta&quot;;

export default defineSeoConfig({
  site: {
    url: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
    name: &quot;My Site&quot;,
    title: &quot;My Site&quot;,
    titleTemplate: &quot;%s | My Site&quot;,
    description: &quot;My awesome site&quot;,
  },
  pages: {
    &quot;/about&quot;: {
      title: &quot;About Us&quot;,
      description: &quot;Who we are and what we build.&quot;,
    },
    &quot;/blog/hello-world&quot;: {
      title: &quot;Hello, world&quot;,
      description: &quot;My first post on this site.&quot;,
      noindex: true,
    },
  },
});

How the puzzle assembles

site holds the defaults every page inherits
pages overrides specific routes. A page can set any field the site has, plus noindex, canonical, alternates and robots
The plugin merges site defaults with the matching page, then outputs tags at build time

25.text:
JSON-LD, also known as JSON Linked Data, is a way to provide structured data to search engines. It is a standard format for describing the content of a web page for better indexing and results.This plugin emits JSON-LD as &lt;script type=&quot;application/ld+json&quot;&gt; blocks. Enable the types you need in site.jsonLd and add per-page data in pages[].jsonLd.

The format is pretty well standardized at json-ld.org and the vocabulary for what you can describe lives at schema.org. The plugin models the common types from schema.org; anything not covered goes through extra! If you feel that any type is missing or should be added to the plugin, please open an issue or PR, I’d love to add it!
Organization
site: {
  jsonLd: { organization: true },
}

This emits an Organization block with the site name and URL. Add a logo:
site: {
  name: &quot;My Site&quot;,
  jsonLd: {
    organization: {
      name: &quot;My Site&quot;,
      logo: &quot;https://example.com/logo.png&quot;,
    },
  },
}

Heads up! This is site-wide config, so it goes on every page. Google recommends putting Organization markup on just the home page, but having it everywhere is still valid, just a bit redundant. If you really only want it once, use extra on that page instead (sigh if you’re really that picky, alright, but I get it lol).
WebSite
site: {
  jsonLd: { website: true },
}

Emits a WebSite block. Set searchUrl to add a search action:
site: {
  jsonLd: {
    website: {
      searchUrl: &quot;https://example.com/search?q={search_term_string}&quot;,
    },
  },
}

That emits a SearchAction with an EntryPoint and query-input. The {search_term_string} bit is required; the validator flags a searchUrl without it, cause nothing could fill the query in otherwise.
Google removed the sitelinks search box feature on November 29, 2024. The markup is still valid schema.org and harmless, so I emit it, but it won’t do a Google feature anymore sadly cause Google keeps killing things.
Article
pages: {
  &quot;/blog/hello-world&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      article: {
        headline: &quot;Hello, world&quot;,
        type: &quot;BlogPosting&quot;,
        author: { name: &quot;Alice&quot; },
        publisher: &quot;Example Inc&quot;,
        datePublished: &quot;2024-01-15&quot;,
        dateModified: &quot;2024-06-20&quot;,
        description: &quot;My first post on this site.&quot;,
        image: &quot;https://example.com/post.png&quot;,
      },
    },
  },
}

Note: author can be a string or an object with name, url and sameAs. Set &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;Organization&quot; on that object for a company author (it defaults to Person). publisher takes the same shapes but defaults to Organization; Google doesn’t require it, so skip it if you don’t have one.
type defaults to Article; set BlogPosting, NewsArticle or the others as needed. Dates are normalized to ISO 8601, so both Date objects and loose strings like &quot;Jan 1 2024&quot; come out (they queer? eyes emoji) as proper timestamps. Anything that doesn’t parse gets dropped rather than emitted as garbage, and validation warns you about it. Google dropped the old hard 110-character headline limit, but the validator still nudges you past that point, cause long headlines get truncated on some devices.
Product
pages: {
  &quot;/products/widget&quot;: {
    jsonLd: {
      product: {
        name: &quot;Widget&quot;,
        image: &quot;https://example.com/widget.png&quot;,
        description: &quot;A widget for doing things.&quot;,
        sku: &quot;WIDGET-001&quot;,
        brand: &quot;My Brand&quot;,
        price: 19.99,
        priceCurrency: &quot;USD&quot;,
        availability: &quot;InStock&quot;,
        ratingValue: 4.8,
        ratingCount: 127,
        reviewCount: 42,
      },
    },
  },
}

Everything is flat here; nothing to build. price can be a number or a string, and 0 is preserved. availability takes a short name like InStock or a full https://schema.org/InStock URL. Even /InStock works, as I (subjectively smartly) strip the leading slash.
Ratings need ratingValue plus at least one of ratingCount or reviewCount to be eligible for rich results. The validator warns if you leave both counts off.
Extra
If the plugin does not cover a type you need, pass raw JSON-LD through extra:
jsonLd: {
  extra: [
    {
      &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;HowTo&quot;,
      name: &quot;How to do the thing&quot;,
      step: [{ &quot;@type&quot;: &quot;HowToStep&quot;, text: &quot;Do the thing&quot; }],
    },
  ],
}

extra is emitted verbatim. It is what you would call an “escape hatch” for types the plugin does not model. BreadcrumbList and ImageObject aren’t built in, so use extra for those. Whenever anything is configured, the plugin will prepend a WebPage node with the page’s title, description, canonical and image. If nothing at all is configured, no script tag is emitted, obviously.
If you feel that any type is missing or should be added to the plugin, please open an issue or PR.

38.text:
Open Graph, also known as og:* tags, is a universal standard for describing how a page looks when shared on social media. It’s used by Facebook, LinkedIn, Discord, Slack (oh hi Hack Club), and many other platforms. Suffice to say, they’re important.
The spec exists at ogp.me.

Side note: In my opinion, that’s how spec pages should be in my opinion, as it’s pretty easy to parse, not 20 pages and written in legalese lol.

They are emitted from the openGraph block, which inherits from site defaults.
Basic tags
pages: {
  &quot;/&quot;: {
    openGraph: {
      title: &quot;My Site&quot;,
      description: &quot;My awesome site&quot;,
      type: &quot;website&quot;,
      url: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
      image: &quot;https://example.com/og.png&quot;,
      siteName: &quot;My Site&quot;,
      locale: &quot;en_US&quot;,
    },
  },
}


type defaults to website. Set article for blog posts, book, profile, music.*, video.* or others… based on what your page is about.
url defaults to the page canonical
image defaults to site.image. There’s no built-in fallback image, so if neither is set the tag is missing entirely, which Open Graph doesn’t allow. Validation warns you about it, so set site.image (or openGraph.image) somewhere please

locale follows language_TERRITORY, so en_US. You can write en-US too, it gets converted, same for localeAlternate entries. Anything that can’t be converted gets flagged by validation and yells at you
Article type
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;article&quot;,
  article: {
    publishedTime: &quot;2024-01-15&quot;,
    modifiedTime: &quot;2024-06-20&quot;,
    author: &quot;https://example.com/authors/alice&quot;,
    section: &quot;Technology&quot;,
    tag: [&quot;typescript&quot;, &quot;vite&quot;],
  },
}

author can be a single URL or an array. tag is the same. Dates should be ISO 8601. Date objects get converted automatically, and strings are checked by validation, cause Open Graph wants an actual time rather than something like “last tuesday” lol.
Book type

openGraph: {
  type: &quot;book&quot;,
  book: {
    author: &quot;https://example.com/authors/popper&quot;,
    isbn: &quot;978-3-16-148410-0&quot;,
    releaseDate: &quot;2019-02-01&quot;,
    tag: [&quot;fiction&quot;],
  },
}

Profile type
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;profile&quot;,
  profile: {
    firstName: &quot;Karl&quot;,
    lastName: &quot;Popper&quot;,
    username: &quot;karlpopper&quot;,
    gender: &quot;male&quot;,
  },
}

Music types
music.album, music.playlist and music.radio_station share the same fields:
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;music.album&quot;,
  music: {
    duration: 213,
    musician: [&quot;https://example.com/artists/queen&quot;],
    song: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/songs/we-will-rock-you&quot;, disc: 1, track: 1 }],
    releaseDate: &quot;1977-10-21&quot;,
  },
}

song and album are arrays of { url, disc?, track? }. disc and track default to 1. duration is a whole number of seconds, at least 1 (NO 0); validation flags zero, negatives and fractions.
Video types
video.movie, video.episode and video.series:
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;video.movie&quot;,
  video: {
    actor: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/people/tom-hanks&quot;, role: &quot;Forrest Gump&quot; }],
    director: [&quot;https://example.com/people/robert-zemeckis&quot;],
    duration: 142 * 60 + 24,
    releaseDate: &quot;1994-06-23&quot;,
    series: &quot;https://example.com/series/futurama&quot;,
  },
}

Structured images, audio and video
Instead of a single image URL, pass structured entries with extra properties:
openGraph: {
  images: [
    { url: &quot;https://example.com/og-1200x630.png&quot;, width: 1200, height: 630, alt: &quot;Share this page&quot; },
  ],
  audios: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/podcast.mp3&quot; }],
  videos: [{ url: &quot;https://example.com/trailer.mp4&quot; }],
}

Structured images override the flat image field when present.
payment.link
openGraph: {
  type: &quot;payment.link&quot;,
  payment: {
    description: &quot;One month subscription&quot;,
    currency: &quot;USD&quot;,
    amount: 9.99,
    status: &quot;PENDING&quot;,
    id: &quot;sub_123&quot;,
    successUrl: &quot;https://example.com/thanks&quot;,
  },
}

determiner
openGraph: {
  determiner: &quot;auto&quot;;
}

Valid values: &quot;a&quot;, &quot;an&quot;, &quot;the&quot;, &quot;auto&quot;, and &quot;&quot; (the default, which omits the tag.). A determiner is the word that comes before the title noun. &quot;auto&quot; lets the platform decide whether or not to include it.

55.text:
A sitemap is an XML file that lists all the pages on your site. It’s extremely useful for search engines, as it’s a easily parsible list of all your pages. It can also include images, videos and news articles.
The protocol is documented at sitemaps.org. I tried to follow this as closely as possible, but if you notice any discrepancies, please open an issue or PR!!!

sitemap.xml is generated at build time from the pages map. It lists every route that is not noindex and not in exclude.
Basic options
sitemap: {
  exclude: [&quot;/admin&quot;, &quot;/login&quot;],
  maxUrls: 100_000,
  defaultChangefreq: &quot;weekly&quot;,
  defaultPriority: 0.8,
  siteUrl: &quot;https://example.com&quot;,
  maxBytes: 50 * 1024 * 1024,
  maxNews: 1_000,
  newsMaxAgeDays: 2,
}


exclude: routes to leave out
maxUrls: URLs per sitemap file. Default 50000. Anything past that gets dropped
defaultChangefreq: fallback for pages without one
defaultPriority: fallback for pages without one. I clamp it to 0.0-1.0, cause the protocol is picky
siteUrl: origin used to enforce a single-host sitemap
maxBytes: uncompressed size cap in bytes. Default 50 MiB. Go over and the build throws, cause a sitemap Google rejects is not much use
maxNews: maximum &lt;news:news&gt; entries per file. Default 1000
newsMaxAgeDays: how old a news article can be. Default 2; 0 turns the check off
now: reference time for the freshness check. Defaults to build time; handy only if you want deterministic builds

changefreq
Per-page or global and valid values: always, hourly, daily, weekly, monthly, yearly, never.
pages: {
  &quot;/blog/hello-world&quot;: { changefreq: &quot;weekly&quot;, priority: 0.9 },
  &quot;/&quot;: { changefreq: &quot;daily&quot;, priority: 1.0 },
}

Priority
The protocol says priorities live between 0.0 and 1.0, so I clamp whatever you pass into that range and drop non-finite values. Pre-build validation also yells about out-of-range page priorities.
maxUrls and file size
Past maxUrls URLs, extra pages are simply skipped. No automatic sitemap-1.xml splitting yet (sorry). The default of 50000 is the sitemaps.org recommendation. Most sites should never hit it. Raise it if you have a large site, but keep an eye on the file size: it has to stay under 50 MiB uncompressed. Go over and buildSitemap() throws!
Image namespace
When a page has openGraph.image (or structured images), the sitemap entry gets &lt;image:image&gt;:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/blog/hello-world&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;lastmod&gt;2024-06-20&lt;/lastmod&gt;
  &lt;changefreq&gt;weekly&lt;/changefreq&gt;
  &lt;priority&gt;0.9&lt;/priority&gt;
  &lt;image:image&gt;
    &lt;image:loc&gt;https://example.com/post.png&lt;/image:loc&gt;
  &lt;/image:image&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

Only &lt;image:loc&gt; these days. Google dropped image:caption, image:title, image:geo_location, and image:license back in 2022. Up to 1,000 images per URL, which is more than enough for even the most trigger-happy image poster lollllll.
Video namespace
When a page has openGraph.videos, the sitemap entry gets &lt;video:video&gt;:
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/watch/trailer&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;video:video&gt;
    &lt;video:thumbnail_loc&gt;https://example.com/thumb.png&lt;/video:thumbnail_loc&gt;
    &lt;video:title&gt;Trailer&lt;/video:title&gt;
    &lt;video:description&gt;Watch the trailer.&lt;/video:description&gt;
    &lt;video:content_loc&gt;https://example.com/trailer.mp4&lt;/video:content_loc&gt;
  &lt;/video:video&gt;
&lt;/url&gt;

A thumbnail is required for video entries! Pages without one are automatically skipped rather than emitting invalid XML.
title and description on the media entry win, then alt, then the page title. Both get chopped at Google’s limits (100 and 2048 characters). Got a YouTube/Vimeo embed instead of an actual media file? Pass playerLoc and it’ll emit player_loc instead of content_loc:
pages: {
  &quot;/watch/trailer&quot;: {
    openGraph: {
      videos: [
        {
          src: &quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=abc&quot;,
          thumbnail: &quot;https://img.youtube.com/vi/abc/hqdefault.jpg&quot;,
          playerLoc: &quot;https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc&quot;,
          title: &quot;Launch recap&quot;,
          description: &quot;Highlights from the launch stream.&quot;,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}

News namespace
When a page sets openGraph.article.publishedTime and openGraph.siteName, the sitemap entry gets a &lt;news:news&gt; block. Google News wants a publication name, language, title, and publication date, so entries missing those get dropped. Articles older than two days get dropped too (Google’s rule, not mine); tune it with newsMaxAgeDays if you must.
&lt;url&gt;
  &lt;loc&gt;https://example.com/blog/breaking&lt;/loc&gt;
  &lt;news:news&gt;
    &lt;news:publication&gt;
      &lt;news:name&gt;My Site&lt;/news:name&gt;
      &lt;news:language&gt;en&lt;/news:language&gt;
    &lt;/news:publication&gt;
    &lt;news:publication_date&gt;2024-0

65.text:
SPA apps have a problem. If you navigate to a new route, the &lt;title&gt; and &lt;meta&gt; tags do not update. This is because the plugin only injects them at build time.So, the plugin injects tags at build time for the initial HTML. When the client navigates to a new route, the tags do not update on their own so you need to call applySeo().
import { applySeo } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta/runtime&quot;;

function onRouteChange() {
  applySeo({ title: &quot;New Page&quot;, description: &quot;...&quot; });
}

How it works
applySeo(page, site?) merges the page data with the injected site config. Pass site explicitly when you need a different site than the one the plugin injected. Omit it and the plugin uses the config from the build.
It only updates head elements the plugin manages and your own tags are left alone once the page is loaded!
What it updates

&lt;title&gt;
&lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot;&gt;
&lt;meta name=&quot;robots&quot;&gt;
&lt;link rel=&quot;canonical&quot;&gt;
Open Graph tags
Twitter Card tags
JSON-LD scripts

When to call it
Call it after every route change. In a router, that is the navigation callback:
// vue-router
router.afterEach((to) =&gt; {
  applySeo({ title: to.meta.title, description: to.meta.description });
});

// react-router
useEffect(() =&gt; {
  applySeo({ title: location.pathname, description: &quot;...&quot; });
}, [location]);

What it does not do
applySeo updates tags. It does not push history, change the URL, or update the browser title bar. Your router handles that! Don’t just rely on applySeo if you’re making an SPA (if that wasn’t obvious already)!
It also does not fetch data, as you should already have it available. Pass the metadata you already have!

70.text:
SvelteKit makes its own HTML, so Vite’s transformIndexHtml never fires and the main plugin just sits there doing nothing. It even warns you about the unsupported hook, which is rude but fair. Use the vite-plugin-seo-meta/sveltekit subpackage for this purpose, which injects through SvelteKit’s own transformPageChunk. Basically the same tags, but with a different entry.
Setup
Keep seo() in your Vite config. It still does config resolution, validation, robots.txt and sitemap.xml. The handle hook only takes over head injection.
// vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from &quot;@sveltejs/kit/vite&quot;;
import { seo } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta&quot;;
import { defineConfig } from &quot;vite&quot;;

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [seo(), sveltekit()],
});

Then add the hook. Import site and pages from the plugin’s virtual config module and pass them to seoHandle():
// src/hooks.server.ts
import { site, pages } from &quot;virtual:vite-plugin-seo-meta/config&quot;;
import { seoHandle } from &quot;vite-plugin-seo-meta/sveltekit&quot;;

export const handle = seoHandle({ site, pages });

That’s it!!! Dev SSR and prerendered builds both go through this hook so it’s an all-in-one solution 😄. Already got a handle hook of your own (niceee I like you if so)? Compose them with SvelteKit’s sequence:
import { sequence } from &quot;@sveltejs/kit/hooks&quot;;

export const handle = sequence(seoHandle({ site, pages }), myOtherHandle);

Base paths
The hook strips the deployment base from event.url.pathname before matching a page key, so /docs/about still finds pages[&quot;/about&quot;]. The base defaults to the pathname of site.url, since that’s usually where a subpath deployment hides it. If SvelteKit’s paths.base disagrees, say so:
export const handle = seoHandle({ site, pages, base: &quot;/docs&quot; });

Dynamic routes
Page keys match the concrete pathname first. Nothing matched? The hook then tries SvelteKit’s event.route.id, so one entry can cover a whole dynamic route (blog posts and the like yk):
// seo.config.ts
export default defineSeoConfig({
  site: { url: &quot;https://example.com&quot; },
  pages: {
    &quot;/blog/[slug]&quot;: { title: &quot;Blog post&quot; },
  },
});

Robots.txt and sitemap.xml
These still come from the plugin’s closeBundle hook, with URLs built from site.url, so they already know about the base. Routes come from built HTML plus the keys in pages, so SSR-only routes need an explicit pages entry if you want them in the sitemap, which I thought was fair:
pages: {
  &quot;/blog/[slug]&quot;: { title: &quot;Blog post&quot; },
  &quot;/dashboard&quot;: { title: &quot;Dashboard&quot; },
},

Notes

The hook buffers the page until SvelteKit marks the last chunk done, then injects once. That trades streaming for correct, non-duplicated tags. Worth it in my opinion, but if you disagree, open an issue and we can discuss it! 😄
Non-HTML responses are left alone

